[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 821,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 764,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128413,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 764,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cellular neurothekeoma is a benign cutaneous neoplasm of uncertain histogenesis. This tumour type is not related to nerve sheath myxoma (formerly &ldquo;myxoid neurothekeoma&rdquo;).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T15:03:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 822,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 765,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128414,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 765,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9562/0 Cellular neurothekeoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T15:03:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 823,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 766,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128415,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 766,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F24 &amp; XH1UZ6 Benign cutaneous neoplasms of neural or nerve sheath origin &amp; Cellular neurothekeoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T15:03:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 824,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 767,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128416,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> neurothekeoma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T15:03:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128417,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T15:03:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128418,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cellular neurothekeoma has a predilection for the face (in particular the nasomalar and nasolabial areas), shoulders, and upper limbs {16327434; 7446487; 17325474; 3297114}. It is usually solitary, although rare examples of multiple lesions have been reported {17592278; 16441413}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T15:03:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128419,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cellular neurothekeoma manifests as a solitary firm papule or nodule with no distinctive clinical features.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T15:03:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128420,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cellular neurothekeoma occurs most commonly in children and young adults, with a female predominance {16327434; 7446487; 17325474; 3297114}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T15:03:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128421,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T15:03:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128422,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The histogenesis is uncertain, but the gene expression profile is similar to that of cellular fibrous histiocytoma, suggesting myofibroblastic differentiation {21297585}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T15:03:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128423,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cellular neurothekeoma appears as a well-circumscribed dermal nodule.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T15:03:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128424,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cellular neurothekeoma is a lobulated or micronodular neoplasm composed of nests of usually epithelioid but occasionally spindled cells with a vesicular nucleus, small nucleolus, and pale eosinophilic cytoplasm, often separated by dense collagenous stroma {17325474}. Some tumours contain prominent myxoid stroma, and rare cases may show an infiltrative growth pattern {29744902}. Occasional neoplastic cells may be multinucleated or may show hyperchromatic nuclei, and mitotic figures are common {1880258; 2154139; 24186141}. In rare cases, areas of calcification {3745622}, ossification {8030774}, perineural invasion {24186141}, and a plexiform pattern with hybrid features of both cellular neurothekeoma and perineurioma can be seen {23598966}.</p>\r\n<h6>Immunohistochemistry</h6>\r\n<p>The most consistent markers expressed in cellular neurothekeoma are CD63 (NKI/C3) {8188941; 33264109}, NSE {7693776}, PGP9.5 {10555009}, and S100A6 {12641790}, as well as KBA.62 and PRAME (these last two are also expressed in melanoma) {24151815; 34761425}. Expression of MITF, SMA, and podoplanin (D2-40) is more variable {19318798; 14685254; 24186141; 33264109}. Cellular neurothekeoma does not express S100, GFAP, CD57, SOX10, or EMA {1880258; 2154139; 3297114; 16327434; 24475873}.</p>\r\n<h6>Differential diagnosis</h6>\r\n<p>The differential diagnosis includes nerve sheath myxoma, Spitz naevus, cutaneous leiomyoma, and plexiform fibrohistiocytic tumour. Nerve sheath myxomas and Spitz naevi are positive for S100. Cutaneous leiomyoma has an intersecting fascicular pattern and is desmin-positive. Plexiform fibrohistiocytic tumour has a biphasic appearance and a plexiform growth pattern.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T15:03:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
